Linux-port DDR stability (development record #387): clk: rockchip: rk3128: keep pclk_pwm on.

PCLK_PWM is the only clock of the RK3128's four PWM channels (rk3128.dtsi gives all four the
same clock). On this board pwm1 and pwm2 drive the CPU and logic rails (pwm-regulator, u-boot
starts them) and pwm0 drives the backlight. In a build without the PWM regulators nothing holds
the clock but pwm-rockchip's backlight channel, so a backlight off (fb blank, DPMS off,
brightness 0) disables the last enable of PCLK_PWM and the CRU gates it: the counters stop
and both rail PWMs freeze at whatever level their output had at that instant, i.e. the CPU and
logic voltages jump to the table's top (1.45 V, output low) or bottom (output high). On the
frame (#387): the enable count went 1 -> 0 at the blank (attempt 387-4), and a regulator-less
boot at 396 MHz reset at the blank (387-2); with this patch it stays at 1 (387-6). Marking the
clock critical keeps it on whatever the consumers do; it costs one APB clock gate.
---
diff --git a/drivers/clk/rockchip/clk-rk3128.c b/drivers/clk/rockchip/clk-rk3128.c
--- a/drivers/clk/rockchip/clk-rk3128.c
+++ b/drivers/clk/rockchip/clk-rk3128.c
@@ -568,6 +568,13 @@
 	"pclk_peri",
 	"pclk_pmu",
 	"sclk_timer5",
+	/*
+	 * The one clock of all four PWMs: pwm1 and pwm2 drive the CPU and logic rails
+	 * (u-boot starts them), pwm0 the backlight. Gated, the counters stop and the
+	 * rail PWMs freeze at their current level: the backlight going off (its PWM
+	 * disabled) took the last enable away when no pwm-regulator held it.
+	 */
+	"pclk_pwm",
 };
 
 static void __iomem *rk3128_cru_base;
